  ],
  "text": "London taxi drivers memorise twenty five thousand streets, and it shows up on a brain scan. The old line was that adult brains are fixed. Set by your twenties, then downhill. The region handling spatial memory is measurably larger in them than in anyone else. And it grows across the years of training, not before it. The job built it. Musicians show the same thing, in the regions controlling the fingers they actually use. But the strongest evidence comes from damage. After a stroke, undamaged regions can take over work they were never assigned. In people blind from birth, the visual cortex gets recruited for touch and hearing instead. Be honest about the price though. It slows with age and it needs sustained, repeated effort. Your brain is not fixed. It is just expensive to change. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
